Complete pooling

pooled_model &lt;- "
data {
  int N; // number of respondents
  int K; // number of covariates
  matrix[N, K] X; // covariate matrix
  array[N] int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; y; // 1 = Clinton, 0 = Trump
  int M; // number of cells
  matrix[M, K] X_cell; // covariates for each cell
  array[M] int n_cell; // number of respondents in each cell
}
parameters {
  vector[K] beta; // regression coefficients
}
model {
  beta ~ normal(0, 5); // normal prior
  y ~ bernoulli_logit(X * beta); // logistic regression
}
generated quantities {
  vector[M] eta_cell = X_cell * beta; // linear predictor for each cell
  array[M] int y_rep = binomial_rng(n_cell, inv_logit(eta_cell)); // replicated Clinton voters in each cell
  vector[M] ll_clinton = log_inv_logit(eta_cell); // log-likelihood of one Clinton voter in each cell
  vector[M] ll_trump = log1m_inv_logit(eta_cell); // log-likelihood of one Trump voter in each cell
}
"

Varying intercepts model

Next, our parameters.

parameters{
  vector[K] beta; // beta coefficients
  vector[J] alpha; // random intercepts
  real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma_a; // standard deviation of intercepts
}

Finally, our model

model{
  beta ~ normal(0, 5); // normal prior on coefficients
  alpha ~ normal(0, sigma_a); // normal distribution on random intercepts
  sigma_a ~ cauchy(0, 2.5); // half-Cauchy, since sigma_a is constrained to be positive
  y ~ bernoulli_logit(alpha[state] + X*beta);
}

Varying intercepts model

Put it into Stan! The generated quantities block computes the same cell-level quantities as before

generated quantities {
  vector[M] eta_cell = alpha[cell_state] + X_cell*beta;
  array[M] int y_rep = binomial_rng(n_cell, inv_logit(eta_cell));
  vector[M] ll_clinton = log_inv_logit(eta_cell);
  vector[M] ll_trump = log1m_inv_logit(eta_cell);
}

model_var_intercept &lt;- stan(
  model_code = varying_intercepts_model,  # Stan code
  data = stan_data,       # named list of data
  chains = 4,             # number of Markov chains
  warmup = 500,           # number of warmup iterations per chain
  iter = 2500,            # total number of iterations per chain
  cores = 4,              # number of cores (this is much slower if = 1)
  refresh = 0,            # no progress shown
  seed = 53706            # random seed
  )

Leave-one-out CV

In order to use loo, we need the log-likelihood of each observation \(\log p(y_i | \theta^s)\), evaluated at each posterior draw \(s\). This comes by default in canned rstanarm or brms models but for custom models we need to specify to return it in the generated quantities block
For our logistic regression, each respondent’s log-likelihood:

generated quantities {
  vector[N] log_lik;
  for (i in 1:N) log_lik[i] = bernoulli_logit_lpmf(y[i] | X[i] * beta);
}

loo() then computes PSIS-LOO from this \(N \times S\) matrix, and loo_compare() compares models by summing the pointwise differences in \(\log p(y_i | y_{-i})\)

Varying slopes model

The parameters block

parameters{
  vector[P] b; // population-level coefficients (here, mu_beta)
  corr_matrix[K] Omega; //prior correlation
  vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[K] tau; //prior scale
  array[J] vector[K] e; // state deviations
}

The model block

model{
  vector[N] eta; // linear predictor for each respondent
  tau ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);
  Omega ~ lkj_corr(2);
  b ~ normal(0, 5);
  e ~ multi_normal(rep_vector(0, K), quad_form_diag(Omega, tau));
  for (i in 1:N) {
    eta[i] = X_fix[i] * b + X[i] * e[state[i]];
  }
  y ~ bernoulli_logit(eta);
}

